# Frame grabs. Diagnosing a camera means saving a still, and the next still
# saved in this folder will be of the perimeter this system watches rather than
# an ffmpeg test pattern. There is no rule that makes that safe to publish, so
# no image in the tree is committed by accident.
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.png
*.bmp
footage/
.superpowers/
yolo11n.pt
label.bat
bench.bat

gui_err.log
gui_out.log
*.bin
flir_saved.html
wb.html
.claude/tdd-guard/
.playwright-mcp/
mockup/*.png

recordings/
settings.json
smoke_record.*

# One folder per camera, made by cameras.bat on the machine this is installed
# on: a settings.json with that camera's address and password in it, its
# recordings, its segment index, and the window it remembers. All of it belongs
# to one installation and none of it belongs in the history. The launcher
# written beside it - "Camera 250.bat" - is generated from the same script, so
# it is not committed either.
#
# The space in the pattern matters. Git on Windows matches these without regard
# to case, so "Camera*.bat" also matched cameras.bat - the one file here that
# has to be committed, because it is how the operator sets a camera up.
cameras/
Camera *.bat

# What a run leaves behind beside the settings. *.db above already covers
# events.db and segments.db; these are the rest of the same set - the ports
# go2rtc took, the config written for it, the state the detector publishes, and
# the PID files the console adopts its children by. Every one of them is written
# by a run and rebuilt by the next, and every one of them carries something
# about the machine it ran on.
go2rtc.json
streaming.json
detection.json
*.pid
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Test and type-checker scratch.
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Downloaded binaries (go2rtc). The installer refetches them.
bin/

# Built artefacts (VMD.exe and PyInstaller scratch). The installer rebuilds them.
dist/
build/
/VMD.exe
